.Dd October 6, 2026
.Dt ZKEYS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zkeys
.Nd retrieve secrets from a zkeys service
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm get-key
.Op Fl -config-file Ar file
.Ar name
.Nm
.Cm keep-alive
.Op Fl -config-file Ar file
.Op Ar name1 .. nameN
.Nm
.Cm list-keys
.Op Fl -config-file Ar file
.Nm
.Cm luks-stage-keys
.Op Fl -config-file Ar file
.Op Fl -output-dir Ar directory
.Nm
.Cm zfs-change-key
.Op Fl -config-file Ar file
.Op Fl -quiet
.Op Ar zfs-arg1 ..
.Ar zfs-argN
.Nm
.Cm zfs-create
.Op Fl -config-file Ar file
.Op Fl -quiet
.Op Ar zfs-arg1 ..
.Ar zfs-argN
.Nm
.Cm zfs-list
.Op Fl -config-file Ar file
.Nm
.Cm zfs-load-key
.Op Fl -config-file Ar file
.Fl a | Ar dataset
.Nm
.Cm zpool-create
.Op Fl -config-file Ar file
.Op Fl -quiet
.Op Ar zpool-arg1 ..
.Ar zpool-argN
.Nm
.Cm help
.Op Ar command
.Nm
.Cm version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
retrieves secrets from a zkeys service and is designed for integration with
encrypted storage to securely unlock volumes, pools, or file systems without
keeping the full key stored on the same machine.
.Pp
Note that, by design, this client does
.Em not
support administrative key operations such as key unlocking.
This is to prevent mistakes resulting in ever storing privileged credentials on
the machine as that would defeat the purpose of the remote service's auto-key
locking features.
.Ss The get-key command
The get-key command retrieves the secret for the key named by
.Ar name .
The name must identify a key in the configuration file.
The client authenticates to the service with the key's configured identifier
and remote password.
It prepends the configured local secret to the service response and writes the
result to standard output, followed by a newline.
.Pp
The following option is supported:
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Fl -config-file Ar file
Path to the configuration file.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
.El
.Ss The list-keys command
The list-keys command prints the names of all keys in the configuration file.
Each key name is printed on its own line, with no other output.
The names are sorted lexicographically.
.Pp
The following option is supported:
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Fl -config-file Ar file
Path to the configuration file.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
.El
.Ss The luks-stage-keys command
The luks-stage-keys command retrieves the keys associated with all configured
LUKS volumes and then atomically writes each key to a file named
.Pa <volume>.key .
This is designed to operate in conjunction with
.Xr dracut 8
to stage keys for unattended unlocking of boot volumes.
.Pp
The command tries to make as much progress as possible if a key cannot be
staged, but reports an error if any key fails or if no LUKS volumes are
configured.
.Pp
The files and any newly-created destination directories are only accessible by
the user running the command.
.Pp
The following options are supported:
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Fl -config-file Ar file
Path to the configuration file.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
.It Fl -output-dir Ar directory
Directory in which to stage the keys.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa /run/cryptsetup-keys.d
.El
.Ss The keep-alive command
The keep-alive command periodically updates the last-access time of keys so
that the service does not automatically lock them.
This command is intended to be run as a system service while the machine is
active so that the keys remain unlocked and are ready for the next boot.
.Pp
If one or more key names are given, they must all identify keys in the
configuration file.
If no names are given, the command operates on all configured keys.
.Pp
Each key is first refreshed immediately and, during the process, the server
returns a recommended refresh period for subsequent requests.
If the service does not provide a refresh period or the keep-alive ping
fails, the value of
.Va default_key_refresh_period
from
.Xr zkeys.toml 5
is used.
.Pp
The following options are supported:
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Fl -config-file Ar file
Path to the configuration file.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
.El
.Ss The zfs-change-key command
The zfs-change-key command retrieves the key associated with the ZFS dataset
named by the final
.Ar zfs-arg
and provides it to the standard input of the following command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs change-key \e
    -o keyformat=passphrase \e
    -o keylocation=prompt \e
    zfs-arg1 .. zfs-argN
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ar zfs-arg
values are passed to
.Xr zfs 8
without interpretation.
The final argument must name a ZFS dataset in the configuration file.
Specify
.Ql --
before these arguments so that options intended for
.Xr zfs 8
are not interpreted as options to
.Nm .
After
.Xr zfs 8
exits successfully, this prints the full key to stderr with a warning about
the requirement to back it up, unless
.Fl -quiet
was specified.
.Pp
The following options are supported:
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Fl -config-file Ar file
Path to the configuration file.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
.It Fl -quiet
Do not print the full key after
.Xr zfs 8
exits successfully.
.El
.Ss The zfs-create command
The zfs-create command retrieves the key associated with the ZFS dataset named
by the final
.Ar zfs-arg
and provides it to the standard input of the following command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs create \e
    -o encryption=on \e
    -o keyformat=passphrase \e
    -o keylocation=prompt \e
    zfs-arg1 .. zfs-argN
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ar zfs-arg
values are passed to
.Xr zfs 8
without interpretation.
The final argument must name a ZFS dataset in the configuration file.
Specify
.Ql --
before these arguments so that options intended for
.Xr zfs 8
are not interpreted as options to
.Nm .
.Pp
After
.Xr zfs 8
exits successfully, this prints the full key to stderr with a warning about
the requirement to back it up, unless
.Fl -quiet
was specified.
.Pp
The following options are supported:
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Fl -config-file Ar file
Path to the configuration file.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
.It Fl -quiet
Do not print the full key after
.Xr zfs 8
exits successfully.
.El
.Ss The zpool-create command
The zpool-create command retrieves the key associated with the root ZFS file
system of the pool named in the
.Ar zpool-arg
arguments and provides it to the standard input of the following command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zpool create \e
    -O encryption=on \e
    -O keyformat=passphrase \e
    -O keylocation=prompt \e
    zpool-arg1 .. zpool-argN
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Fl O
options set file system properties on the pool's root file system so that
descendant file systems inherit encryption by default.
The
.Ar zpool-arg
values are passed to
.Xr zpool 8
without modification.
The pool name following the
.Cm zpool create
options must be a ZFS dataset in the configuration file.
Specify
.Ql --
before these arguments so that options intended for
.Xr zpool 8
are not interpreted as options to
.Nm .
After
.Xr zpool 8
exits successfully, this prints the full key to stderr with a warning about
the requirement to back it up, unless
.Fl -quiet
was specified.
.Pp
The following options are supported:
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Fl -config-file Ar file
Path to the configuration file.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
.It Fl -quiet
Do not print the full key after
.Xr zpool 8
exits successfully.
.El
.Ss The zfs-load-key command
The zfs-load-key command retrieves a configured key and loads it into the ZFS
dataset associated with it.
If the key is already loaded, the command succeeds without retrieving it.
.Pp
The command invokes
.Cm zfs load-key -L prompt
and supplies the key via standard input.
This overrides the dataset's
.Va keylocation
property for the invocation without modifying it.
The dataset's configured
.Va keyformat
still determines how ZFS interprets and validates the key.
.Pp
The following options are supported:
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Fl a , Fl -all
Load keys for all ZFS datasets in the configuration file.
The command tries to make as much progress even if a key cannot be loaded, but
reports an error if any key fails to load.
This option cannot be combined with a dataset argument and fails if no ZFS
datasets are configured.
.It Fl -config-file Ar file
Path to the configuration file.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
.El
.Ss The zfs-list command
The zfs-list command prints the ZFS datasets in the configuration file and the
keys associated with them.
Each line contains a dataset name, one space, and its key name.
The lines are sorted lexicographically by dataset name.
.Pp
The following option is supported:
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Fl -config-file Ar file
Path to the configuration file.
.Pp
Default:
.Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
.El
.Ss The help command
The help command prints command-line usage information.
If
.Ar command
is given, it prints usage information for that command.
.Ss The version command
The version command prints the name of the package and its version to standard
output.
This command always exits successfully.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width XXXX
.It Pa @PREFIX@/etc/zkeys.toml
Default configuration file.
See
.Xr zkeys.toml 5
for its format.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zkeys.toml 5
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
package was developed by
.An Julio Merino
.Aq julio@meroh.net .
